Sure! Here's a simple example of a backend filtering function and API endpoint using Node.js and Express:

1. Install the required dependencies:
```bash
npm init -y
npm install express body-parser
```

2. Create a file named `app.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');

const app = express();
app.use(bodyParser.json());

// Sample hotel data
const hotels = [
  { id: 1, name: 'Hotel A' },
  { id: 2, name: 'Hotel B' },
  { id: 3, name: 'Hotel C' },
  { id: 4, name: 'Hotel D' },
  { id: 5, name: 'Hotel E' },
];

// Backend filtering function
const filterHotels = (hotelName) => {
  return hotels.filter(hotel => hotel.name.toLowerCase().includes(hotelName.toLowerCase()));
};

// API endpoint
app.get('/api/hotels', (req, res) => {
  const hotelName = req.query.name;
  const filteredHotels = filterHotels(hotelName);
  res.json(filteredHotels);
});

// Start the server
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server running on port ${PORT}`));
```

3. Run the server:
```bash
node app.js
```

Now, let's create a simple front-end component for the autocomplete feature:

1. Create an `index.html` file with the following content:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Hotel Autocomplete</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <div class="container">
    <input type="text" id="hotel-search" placeholder="Search hotel...">
    <ul id="hotel-results"></ul>
  </div>
  <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

2. Create a `style.css` file with the following content:

```css
body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
}

.container {
  max-width: 600px;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

input[type="text"] {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px;
  margin-bottom: 10px;
}

ul {
  list-style: none;
  padding: 0;
}

li {
  background-color: #f2f2f2;
  margin-bottom: 5px;
  padding: 10px;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

3. Create a `script.js` file with the following content:

```javascript
const searchInput = document.getElementById('hotel-search');
const resultsList = document.getElementById('hotel-results');

searchInput.addEventListener('input', () => {
  const hotelName = searchInput.value;
  fetch(`/api/hotels?name=${encodeURIComponent(hotelName)}`)
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(hotels => {
      resultsList.innerHTML = '';
      hotels.forEach(hotel => {
        const li = document.createElement('li');
        li.textContent = hotel.name;
        li.addEventListener('click', () => {
          searchInput.value = hotel.name;
          resultsList.innerHTML = '';
        });
        resultsList.appendChild(li);
      });
    });
});
```

Now, you have a simple autocomplete system for searching hotels by name. The backend filtering function and API endpoint are implemented in Node.js, and the front-end component is implemented using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.
